WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.279
Borrowing only makes sense against the
background of ownership.

2
00:00:03.359 --> 00:00:04.218
A value has an owner,

3
00:00:04.298 --> 00:00:07.431
and that owner is responsible for cleanup
when the value goes out of scope.

4
00:00:07.511 --> 00:00:10.796
Borrowing lets another part of the program
use the value temporarily without

5
00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:11.735
becoming the owner.

6
00:00:11.815 --> 00:00:14.645
The original owner remains responsible for
the value's lifetime.

7
00:00:14.725 --> 00:00:16.746
A shared reference provides read-only
access.

8
00:00:16.826 --> 00:00:19.858
A function that receives a shared
reference is saying it can do its job by

9
00:00:19.938 --> 00:00:20.898
inspecting the value.

10
00:00:20.978 --> 00:00:24.061
It does not need to store the value
permanently, consume it, or mutate it.

11
00:00:24.141 --> 00:00:27.627
That keeps the caller's ownership intact
and makes the function easier to compose

12
00:00:27.707 --> 00:00:28.415
with other code.

13
00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.870
A mutable reference provides controlled
write access.

14
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.336
It lets a function change a value owned
somewhere else without taking permanent

15
00:00:34.416 --> 00:00:35.780
responsibility for that value.

16
00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:39.347
This is useful for updating state,
appending to buffers, modifying collections,

17
00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:42.913
and performing transformations where the
caller should keep the same owned value

18
00:00:42.993 --> 00:00:43.499
afterward.

19
00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:45.448
Function signatures are access contracts.

20
00:00:45.528 --> 00:00:49.015
A parameter of an owned type says the
function takes responsibility for the value.

21
00:00:49.095 --> 00:00:51.470
A shared reference says the function needs
read access.

22
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:54.683
A mutable reference says the function
needs to change something in place.

23
00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:58.199
These choices tell callers and reviewers
what kind of relationship the function

24
00:00:58.279 --> 00:00:58.734
requires.

25
00:00:58.814 --> 00:01:01.644
Defensive Rust code uses the least
powerful form that fits the job.

26
00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:04.352
If a function only needs to read, it
should not take ownership.

27
00:01:04.432 --> 00:01:05.796
If it needs temporary mutation,

28
00:01:05.876 --> 00:01:08.352
it should not require a clone or a full
ownership transfer.

29
00:01:08.432 --> 00:01:12.020
Precise borrowing reduces unnecessary
copies and makes data flow easier to review.
